.TH EVENT_LOOP_CREATE 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
event_loop_create \- create an event_loop with default memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_loop_create(max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                  num_reactor_threads, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Convenience macro equivalent to event_loop_create_with_mprocs() with
mmgmt_procs = NULL (standard malloc/free).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the epoll_wait batch buffer each reactor
                      thread uses (must be >= 1).  Bounds how many ready
                      events a single epoll_wait call drains, not the
                      number of registrations the loop can hold.
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the fd/entry
                      registry (must be >= 1).  1 means a single
                      shared lock; pass a larger value to reduce
                      event_loop_add/event_loop_remove/event_loop_modify
                      contention across many different fds/registrations
                      under concurrent use.
num_reactor_threads  Total OS thread count devoted to this loop's own
                      polling and dispatch (must be >= 1); exactly one
                      dedicated thread ever calls epoll_wait on the
                      shared epoll instance regardless of this value.
                      1 means that one thread also dispatches every
                      callback inline; a larger value adds
                      (num_reactor_threads - 1) separate dispatch
                      worker threads.
err_str               Optional pointer to receive an error string on
                      failure (pass NULL to ignore).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new event_loop handle on success.
Returns EVENT_LOOP_INVALID on failure (invalid arguments, allocation
failure, epoll_create1/eventfd/pthread_create failure, or failure to
allocate a handle slot).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Every one of the returned event_loop's background reactor threads is
already running by the time this call returns.  Destroy with
event_loop_destroy() when done.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR event_loop_add (3)
